A||||1||mbramble||Interesting Problem With Radiator Fan||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||07-09-2001||08:53 PM||||When I went out to install the "new" junkyard doors on my 85 GT yesterday, I found the battery was dead.  Since I had no doors, I had put clamps on the door pin switches to keep the lights off.  Somehow one of them got knocked off and drained the battery.  After installing the doors, I jumped the car and let it run for quite awhile to recharge it.  I noticed that the temp got very hot.  The radiator fan nor the fan in the engine compartment came on.  I turned on the AC to get the fans to come on but they didn't.  Finally jumped across the connector at the front fan relay to get it running and cooled the car right down (104 heat index!).  Today I started tracing wires to try and find the problem.  Just concentrated on the rear fan.  Pulled it completely out to find that it did indeed work if it had ground and 12V.  Appeared as if I wasn't getting a ground.  Finally went to the AC/heater control unit and checked that I had a ground in on the right pin but never a ground out when the AC was turned on. Turned out that the control unit was bad and wasn't passing the ground out to the two fans.  Happened to have another one that I picked up at the u-pull-it.  It tested good so I installed it and both fans now come on when the AC is turned on.  Don't know yet if they will come on by themselves in an overheating condition.  Will check that another day!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||07-10-2001||02:46 AM||||I had the same thing about two years ago. I found a burnt circuit in the a/c head.||||reg||1
